{"pages":["Page | 1 @cyberdefentech training@cyberdefentech.com +91 8448046612 C Y B E R S E C U R I T Y T R A I N I N G N O T E S SIEM & Log Analysis Complete Beginner-to-Intermediate Training Notes Learn how security teams collect, correlate and investigate log data to detect threats, triage alerts and respond to incidents, with a safe hands-on lab and a real-world scenario. T0 NETWORK Systems generate activity → T1 LOGS Events are recorded → T2 ALERTS Rules detect patterns → T3 INVESTIGATIO N Analysts validate → T4 RESPONSE Contain and recover SAMPLE SOC DASHBOARD ( ILLUSTRATIVE DATA) EVENTS INGESTED 2.4 M OPEN ALERTS 37 CRITICAL 3 MEAN TIME TO DETECT 14 min INSIDE THIS MODULE 01 Fundamentals 02 Core Concepts 03 How It Works 04 Tools & Techniques 05 Hands-On Lab 06 Real-World Scenario 07 Best Practices 08 Quick Revision MODULE 08 | VERSION 1.0 | FOR AUTHORIZED LAB USE ONLY mailto:training@cyberdefentech.com","Page | 2 @cyberdefentech training@cyberdefentech.com +91 8448046612 01 FUNDAMENTALS What a SIEM is, why it exists and who relies on it DEFINITION What is a SIEM? SIEM (Security Information and Event Management) is a platform that collects log data from across an organization, converts it into a common format, correlates events to detect threats, and gives analysts one place to search, investigate and report. It merges two older ideas: SIM (long-term log storage and reporting) and SEM (real-time monitoring and alerting). IN SIMPLE WORDS Think of a SIEM as the control room of a building full of security cameras. Every server, firewall and laptop keeps a diary (its logs). The SIEM reads all the diaries at once, spots suspicious patterns that no single diary shows, and rings the alarm. WHERE: SOCs, enterprises, cloud, MSSPs, banks, hospitals, government WHO: SOC analysts (L1-L3), incident responders, threat hunters, detection engineers, auditors WHY LEARN: Log analysis is the daily core skill of blue-team and SOC roles CONCEPT DIAGRAM: HOW A SIEM SITS IN THE ENVIRONMENT LOG SOURCES Endpoints | Servers Firewalls | Cloud Identity systems → SIEM PLATFORM 1 Collect 2 Normalize 3 Correlate 4 Alert → OUTPUTS Alerts Dashboards Reports & evidence KEY TERMINOLOGY TERM ONE-LINE DEFINITION Log A timestamped record of something that happened on a system, device or application. Event One normalized log entry stored in the SIEM, such as a single failed login. Alert A notification raised when events match a detection rule or an anomaly. Normalization Converting logs from different vendors into one common field format. Correlation Linking events by user, IP or time to reveal a pattern no single event shows. False positive An alert that looks malicious but is actually harmless activity. Retention How long logs stay stored and searchable; set by policy and compliance. WHY IT MATTERS Visibility One searchable view across thousands of systems instead of logging in to each. Faster detection Correlation joins scattered clues into one alert and cuts time to detect. Evidence & compliance Stored logs support forensics and audits (PCI DSS, ISO 27001, CERT-In 180-day rule). Measurable security Dashboards track alert volume, false positives and response times. KEY TAKEAWAY A SIEM does not create security by itself. It turns raw logs into context, alerts and evidence that a trained analyst can act on. The quality of log sources and the skill of the analyst decide its value. mailto:training@cyberdefentech.com","Page | 3 @cyberdefentech training@cyberdefentech.com +91 8448046612 02 CORE CONCEPTS Six ideas every log analyst must understand 1 LOG SOURCES WHAT Any system that records activity: endpoints, servers, firewalls, DNS, proxies, identity providers, cloud and apps. EXAMPLE Windows Security log writes Event ID 4625 for each failed logon. SECURITY RELEVANCE Missing sources are blind spots. Coverage comes first. 2 LOG COLLECTION WHAT Moving logs to the SIEM using agents, syslog forwarding, APIs or cloud connectors. EXAMPLE A Linux server forwards auth.log through rsyslog to the SIEM collector. SECURITY RELEVANCE Unsynced clocks or silent sources break timelines. Use NTP and health checks. 3 PARSING & NORMALIZATION WHAT Parsers pull fields out of raw text and map them to a common schema. EXAMPLE \"Failed password for root from 203.0.113.9\" becomes user=root, src_ip=203.0.113.9, action=failure. SECURITY RELEVANCE One query works across vendors. Bad parsing hides attacks. 4 CORRELATION & DETECTION RULES WHAT Rules link events over time and entities to flag attack patterns or anomalies. EXAMPLE 10 failed logons then a success from one IP within 10 minutes raises an alert. SECURITY RELEVANCE Map rules to MITRE ATT&CK, for example T1110 Brute Force. 5 ALERT TRIAGE WHAT Reviewing each alert for severity, validity and impact, then closing, escalating or investigating. EXAMPLE A scheduled vulnerability scan trips a port-scan rule; add a documented exception. SECURITY RELEVANCE Alert fatigue hides real incidents. Triage quality matters as much as detection. 6 RETENTION & SEARCH WHAT Storing logs in tiers (hot, warm, cold) so recent data is fast and old data is cheap. EXAMPLE 90 days hot for investigations, 12 months archived for compliance. SECURITY RELEVANCE Intruders can stay for weeks. Short retention erases evidence. FROM NOISE TO INCIDENTS AND KEY CONCEPTS TO REMEMBER RAW LOGS millions per day EVENTS parsed and normalized ALERTS rule or anomaly matches INCIDENTS confirmed threats 1 Cover the right log sources first 2 Time sync (NTP, UTC) is mandatory 3 Normalize once, query everywhere 4 Detections need constant tuning 5 Context turns events into evidence 6 Retention sets what you can investigate COMPARISON: LOG MANAGEMENT vs SIEM ASPECT LOG MANAGEMENT SIEM Main goal Collect, store and search logs Detect threats and support investigations Intelligence Basic search and filtering Correlation, threat intelligence, behavior analytics Typical output Searchable archive and reports Prioritized alerts, cases and dashboards mailto:training@cyberdefentech.com","Page | 4 @cyberdefentech training@cyberdefentech.com +91 8448046612 03 HOW IT WORKS The five-step workflow from raw event to response EVENT → COLLECTION → ANALYSIS → DETECTION → INVESTIGATION → RESPONSE S TE P 01 COLLECT WHAT HAPPENS Agents, syslog and APIs forward logs from endpoints, servers, firewalls, cloud and identity systems. ANALYST ACTION Confirms every source is onboarded, time-synced and not silently failing. DATA COLLECTED Windows Event Logs, Linux auth logs, firewall, DNS, proxy, VPN, cloud audit logs. EXPECTED OUTCOME Complete, timestamped raw events arriving in one place. ↓ S TE P 02 NORMALIZE & ENRICH WHAT HAPPENS Parsers split raw lines into fields (src_ip, user, action) and map them to a common schema. ANALYST ACTION Checks field parsing and adds context such as asset owner, geo-IP and threat intelligence. DATA COLLECTED Parsed fields, asset inventory, user identity, IP reputation feeds. EXPECTED OUTCOME Searchable, comparable events across different vendors. ↓ S TE P 03 DETECT WHAT HAPPENS Correlation rules, thresholds and analytics compare events with known attack patterns and baselines. ANALYST ACTION Writes, tests and tunes detection rules, often mapped to MITRE ATT&CK. DATA COLLECTED Rule matches, event counts, anomaly scores, indicator (IOC) hits. EXPECTED OUTCOME Prioritized alerts with severity and context. ↓ S TE P 04 INVESTIGATE WHAT HAPPENS The analyst triages the alert, pivots through related logs and builds a timeline. ANALYST ACTION Separates true from false positives; checks user, host, IP, process and time window. DATA COLLECTED Related events, authentication history, process and network activity. EXPECTED OUTCOME A verdict (benign, suspicious or confirmed) backed by evidence. ↓ S TE P 05 RESPOND & IMPROVE WHAT HAPPENS Contain, eradicate and recover; escalate to incident response if confirmed. ANALYST ACTION Follows the playbook, documents findings and feeds lessons back into rules. DATA COLLECTED Case notes, containment actions, final timeline, new IOCs. EXPECTED OUTCOME Threat contained, evidence preserved, detections improved. PRO TIP When an alert fires, widen the time window to 30-60 minutes before and after it. The first suspicious event is often not the one that triggered the alert. DID YOU KNOW? Detection quality depends on clean timestamps. A clock drift of even a few minutes can break a correlation rule. WHERE THIS FITS IN FRAMEWORKS NIST CSF 2.0: Detect (continuous monitoring, adverse event analysis) and Respond. MITRE ATT&CK: detections map to techniques. Cyber Kill Chain: shows which attack stage your logs can reveal. mailto:training@cyberdefentech.com","Page | 5 @cyberdefentech training@cyberdefentech.com +91 8448046612 04 TOOLS & TECHNIQUES Platforms, log sources and analysis methods TOOL / TECHNOLOGY PURPOSE BASIC USAGE SECURITY RELEVANCE Splunk Enterprise Security Commercial SIEM that indexes machine data; searched with SPL. Search an index, aggregate with stats, save as a correlation search. Common in SOCs; strong for hunting and alert triage. Microsoft Sentinel Cloud-native SIEM and SOAR on Azure; queried with KQL. Enable data connectors, write analytics rules, review incidents. Natural fit for Microsoft 365, Entra ID and Azure. Elastic Security SIEM built on the Elastic Stack and Kibana. Ingest with Elastic Agent, search in Kibana, enable prebuilt rules. Flexible rules and very fast search at scale. Wazuh Open-source SIEM and XDR platform with host agents. Install server, enrol agents, review events in the dashboard. Free for labs; file integrity and vulnerability detection. Sysmon Windows endpoint telemetry source (Sysinternals). Install with a config file; events land in the Windows log. Rich process, network and file events for detection. Sigma Vendor-neutral detection rule format written in YAML. Write or reuse a rule, convert it for your SIEM. Share and reuse detections across platforms. CORE LOG ANALYSIS TECHNIQUES FILTER Narrow by time, host, user or event type. PIVOT Jump from one indicator to related logs. BASELINE Learn normal to spot abnormal. STACK Count values and review the rare ones. TIMELINE Order events to tell the story. TERMINAL PRACTICE (READ-ONLY, ON YOUR OWN LAB VM) ubuntu-lab: ~ # 1. Recent SSH failures $ sudo grep \"Failed password\" \\ /var/log/auth.log | tail -5 # 2. Which IPs fail most often? $ grep \"Failed password\" /var/log/auth.log \\ | awk '{print $(NF-3)}' | sort \\ | uniq -c | sort -nr | head # 3. Service log from last hour $ journalctl -u ssh --since \"1 hour ago\" \\ | tail -20 SPL (Splunk) index=linux \"Failed password\" | stats count by src_ip | where count > 10 | sort - count KQL (Microsoft Sentinel) SecurityEvent | where EventID == 4625 | summarize Fails=count() by IpAddress, bin(TimeGenerated,5m) | where Fails > 10 Reading the commands: command 1 shows raw evidence; command 2 counts failures per source IP (the IP is the 4th field from the end of an sshd failure line); command 3 uses the systemd journal. SPL and KQL express the same idea: count failures per source and keep only those above a threshold. KEY WINDOWS SECURITY EVENT IDs TO KNOW 4624 Successful logon 4625 Failed logon 4688 Process created 4720 User account created 4732 Member added to local group 1102 Audit log cleared PRO TIP Safe by design: every example here only reads logs you own. Never run searches, scans or log tests against systems you do not own or have written permission to test. mailto:training@cyberdefentech.com","Page | 6 @cyberdefentech training@cyberdefentech.com +91 8448046612 05 HANDS-ON LAB Detect failed SSH logins in an isolated Wazuh lab LAB OBJECTIVE Collect authentication logs from a test server into a SIEM, find a burst of failed SSH logins followed by a success, and document the finding like an analyst. LAB REQUIREMENTS • Hypervisor: VirtualBox, VMware or Proxmox • 2 x Ubuntu Server VMs (siem-lab, target) • siem-lab: about 4 vCPU, 8 GB RAM, 50 GB disk • Host-only or internal virtual network • Snapshots taken before you start LAB ENVIRONMENT: Local isolated VMs | Ubuntu Server | Wazuh all-in-one ONLINE OPTIONS: TryHackMe SOC Level 1 | HTB Academy SOC Analyst | any isolated VM lab STEP-BY-STEP PROCEDURE 01 S TE P Build the isolated lab Create siem-lab and target on a host-only network with no inbound internet access. Take a snapshot of each. $ ip -br a 02 S TE P Install Wazuh on siem-lab Use the all-in-one installer from the official Wazuh Quickstart page. Save the admin password it prints. $ sudo bash ./wazuh-install.sh -a 03 S TE P Enrol target as an agent In the dashboard open Agents, then Deploy new agent. Paste the generated command on target and confirm the status is Active. $ sudo systemctl status wazuh-agent 04 S TE P Generate safe test events On target install openssh-server. From siem-lab try 6 SSH logins with a wrong password for your lab user, then 1 correct login. $ ssh labuser@TARGET_IP 05 S TE P Find and analyze Open Threat Hunting (Events), filter by the target agent, search for sshd. Build a timeline and compare with a threshold of more than 5 failures in 5 minutes. > agent.name: target AND sshd EXPECTED RESULT (SCREENSHOT PLACEHOLDER) TIME AGENT SRC IP RULE DESCRIPTION 10:02:11 target 10.0.2.10 sshd: authentication failed 10:02:14 target 10.0.2.10 sshd: authentication failed 10:02:19 target 10.0.2.10 sshd: authentication failed 10:02:31 target 10.0.2.10 sshd: authentication success 10:02:33 target 10.0.2.10 PAM: session opened HANDS-ON TASK Raise the number of wrong passwords to 12, then write a rule idea (in plain words) that alerts when a success follows more than 5 failures from one IP. LAB SAFETY Perform this lab only on systems you own or are explicitly authorized to test. Keep the lab network isolated and never point test traffic at real or third-party systems. WHAT YOU LEARNED ✓ How agents ship logs to a SIEM ✓ How to read failed vs successful logons ✓ How thresholds turn events into detections ✓ How to document a finding with evidence mailto:training@cyberdefentech.com","Page | 7 @cyberdefentech training@cyberdefentech.com +91 8448046612 06 REAL-WORLD SCENARIO Case study: VPN credential compromise at a logistics firm SCENARIO (F ICT IONAL ORGANIZAT ION) A company notices unusual security activity. At Northwind Logistics, the SIEM raises a critical alert at 10:49: many failed VPN logins from one external IP, followed by a successful login for a finance employee who is on leave. 1 WHAT HAPPENED An attacker guessed a finance user's VPN password after about 40 attempts, logged in and began exploring internal file servers. 2 WHAT TRIGGE RED THE ALERT Correlation rule: 10+ failed VPN logins then a success from the same external IP within 10 minutes (ATT&CK T1110). 3 EVIDENCE AVAI LABLE VPN authentication logs, Windows events 4624/4625/4688, EDR process telemetry, geo-IP and threat-intelligence lookups. 4 HOW THE ANALYST INVESTIGATED Pivoted on username and source IP, confirmed the employee was offline, built a timeline and found PowerShell and file-share enumeration. 5 ROOT CAUSE A password reused from an unrelated breached website, and a VPN that accepted password-only login (no MFA). 6 RESPONSE TAKE N Disabled the account, ended VPN sessions, blocked the IP, isolated the laptop, reset credentials and hunted for the same IP elsewhere. 7 PREVENTING RE CURRENCE Enforce MFA, screen for breached passwords, add lockout and rate limits, tighten geo rules and add post-login recon detections. EVIDENCE AND ATT&CK MAPPING evidence.log (sample) 10:42:11 VPN FAIL m.rao 203.0.113.50 10:42:14 VPN FAIL m.rao 203.0.113.50 $ ... 38 more failures in 6 min 10:48:52 VPN OK m.rao 203.0.113.50 10:51:07 WIN 4688 powershell.exe host=FIN-PC-07 10:53:30 WIN 4624 type=3 host=FS-01 MITRE ATT&CK MAPPING T1110 Brute Force T1078 Valid Accounts T1133 External Remote Services REAL-WORLD RISK Valid credentials let attackers blend in as normal users. Without MFA, one reused password can expose finance data. INCIDENT TIMEL INE T0 10:42 EVENT Failed logons begin T1 10:49 DETECTION Correlation alert fires T2 10:55 INVESTIGATION Timeline and scope T3 11:10 CONTAINMENT Block, isolate, reset T4 +24 h RECOVERY Restore and monitor T5 +14 d PREVENTION MFA and tuning THINK L IKE A SECURITY PROFESSIONAL 1. What evidence would you collect first, and from which log sources? 2. Which indicators would you validate before declaring this an incident (user location, device, IP reputation)? 3. Which single action would reduce the risk fastest? mailto:training@cyberdefentech.com","Page | 8 @cyberdefentech training@cyberdefentech.com +91 8448046612 07 BEST PRACTICES Habits that work, mistakes to avoid and a checklist BEST PRACTICES 1 Define use cases first Start from threats and ATT&CK techniques that matter to your organization. 2 Onboard high-value sources Identity, endpoints, firewalls, DNS and cloud audit logs come first. 3 Synchronize time Use NTP everywhere and store timestamps in UTC. 4 Normalize and document Keep one field naming standard and record parser changes. 5 Tune regularly Track false-positive rates and retire or fix noisy rules. 6 Enrich every alert Add asset criticality, user role and threat intelligence. 7 Protect the logs Restrict access, forward off-host and use integrity controls. 8 Test detections safely Simulate in a lab and review rules after each incident. COMMON BEGINNER MISTAKES ! Collecting everything, analyzing nothing Cost and noise grow while detection stays weak. ! Ignoring log source health A silent source looks exactly like a quiet network. ! Treating every alert as an incident Validate the evidence before escalating. ! Never tuning rules Alert fatigue buries the real attack. ! Looking only at the alert Pivot to events before and after it. ! Poor documentation Unwritten findings cannot be reused or audited. DO vs DON'T DO DON'T ✓ Use authorized labs ✗ Test unauthorized systems ✓ Validate evidence ✗ Assume every alert is malicious ✓ Document findings ✗ Ignore investigation notes ✓ Pivot on user, host and IP ✗ Stop at the first log line ✓ Tune with documented exceptions ✗ Silently disable noisy rules ✓ Preserve original logs ✗ Edit or delete evidence SECURITY CHECKLIST ☐ Understand the fundamentals ☐ Know the key terminology ☐ Practice in an authorized environment ☐ Validate findings ☐ Document evidence ☐ Follow security procedures ☐ Review lessons learned SECURITY ALERT Never disable logging or clear logs \"to reduce noise\". Attackers clear logs (Windows Event ID 1102) to hide their tracks, and a sudden gap in log data is itself an alert. Investigate silent sources immediately. mailto:training@cyberdefentech.com","Page | 9 @cyberdefentech training@cyberdefentech.com +91 8448046612 08 QUICK REVISION Revise, test yourself and prepare for interviews QUICK REVISION 01 SIEM = collect, normalize, correlate, alert and investigate. 02 Log, event, alert and incident form a funnel of rising importance. 03 Coverage and log health matter more than the number of rules. 04 NTP and UTC timestamps make correlation possible. 05 Normalization lets one query work across vendors. 06 Good detections describe attacker behavior (MITRE ATT&CK). 07 Triage means validate, scope, then decide. Never assume malicious. 08 Pivot on user, host, IP, process and time window. 09 Tune continuously to fight alert fatigue. 10 Document everything, preserve evidence, practice only in authorized labs. KEY TERMS TERM MEANING TERM MEANING IOC Indicator of compromise: an IP, hash or domain linked to an intrusion. Playbook Documented steps for handling a type of alert or incident. SOC Security Operations Center: the team that monitors and responds. MTTD Mean time to detect: how long threats go unnoticed. SOAR Tooling that automates response steps with playbooks. MTTR Mean time to respond or recover after detection. UEBA User and entity behavior analytics; flags unusual behavior. Triage Quickly judging an alert's validity and priority. Syslog Standard protocol for forwarding log messages. Pivot Moving from one indicator to related log data. INTERVIEW QUEST IONS AND MODEL ANSWERS Q1 BEGINNER What is a SIEM and what problem does it solve? A platform that collects, normalizes and correlates logs from many sources to detect threats and support investigations. It removes the lack of central visibility. Q2 BEGINNER What is the difference between a log, an event and an alert? A log is a raw record, an event is a parsed entry inside the SIEM, and an alert is a notification raised when events match a rule or anomaly. Q3 BEGINNER Why does time synchronization matter? Correlation and timelines depend on accurate timestamps. Clock drift can break detections and mislead an investigation. Use NTP and UTC. Q4 INTERMEDIATE What does Windows Event ID 4625 mean and how do you use it? It records a failed logon. Count by user and source IP over time to spot brute force or password spraying, then check for a following 4624 success. Q5 INTERMEDIATE How do you reduce false positives without losing detection? Tune thresholds, add documented allowlists, enrich with asset and user context, and test every change against historical data. Q6 INTERMEDIATE A log source suddenly stops sending data. What do you check? Agent or service status, network path and firewall, credentials or API tokens, parser errors and quotas. Then back-fill gaps and add a health alert. Q7 SCENARIO A VPN login succeeds from a new country right after many failures. First steps? Validate with the user, check IP reputation, review session activity and other logins from that IP. If suspicious, disable the account, end sessions and escalate per playbook. Q8 SCENARIO With a small budget, which log sources do you onboard first? Prioritize by risk: identity (AD or Entra ID), endpoints/EDR, firewall and VPN, DNS and proxy, critical servers and cloud audit logs, mapped to use cases. mailto:training@cyberdefentech.com"]}